@page "/privacy"
@model Selficient.Web.Pages.PrivacyModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "How your data is handled";
    ViewData["Description"] = "What leaves your browser, where it goes and why. Short, because there is not much to say.";
    ViewData["Canonical"] = "https://selficient.app/privacy";
    var gb = System.Globalization.CultureInfo.GetCultureInfo("en-GB");
}

<div class="legal-page">
  <header class="legal__head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1 id="privacy-heading">How your data is handled</h1>
    <p class="legal__lede">Selficient needs no account, runs no analytics and keeps no record of who you are unless you choose to make an account. This page is short because there is not much to say.</p>
  </header>

  @await Html.PartialAsync("_LegalRails", Selficient.Web.Pages.LegalRails.Privacy)

  <article class="legal" aria-labelledby="privacy-heading">
    <h2 id="stays">What stays in your browser</h2>
    <p>Your answers about your home, your plan, your ticks, the pin you put on the map, the household id the photo page makes up, and your own Anthropic key if you choose to keep one. They live in your browser's own storage for this site and nowhere else. "Start again" removes the plan; your browser's own settings remove everything. "Export as a file" gives you a copy you control.</p>

    <h2 id="leaves">What leaves your browser, and where it goes</h2>
    <p>Each of these happens only when you do the thing that needs it, and nothing is kept afterwards.</p>
    <div class="legal__scroll">
      <table class="legal__table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Service</th><th scope="col">What it receives</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          @foreach (var (service, receives, purpose) in Selficient.Web.Pages.LegalCopy.Upstreams)
          {
            <tr><td>@service</td><td>@receives</td><td>@purpose</td></tr>
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Where we point to a shop, following the link takes you to that shop, which handles your data under its own terms.</p>

    <h2 id="account">If you make an account</h2>
    <p>You never have to. Everything on Selficient works without one, and your plan is saved on your device either way. An account exists for two things: to keep every device you sign in on in step, and to keep your plan if a device is lost.</p>
    <p>If you make one, we keep your email address and a copy of your plan, and nothing else about you: no name, no password, no profile. The one addition is a street group, if you choose to join one, which has a part of this page to itself. The copy of your plan is encrypted where it is stored. You sign in with a six-digit code sent to your address, which @Selficient.Web.Pages.LegalCopy.MailProvider delivers for us; they receive your address and the message, and nothing about your plan. If you ask for a code and never use it, the request is forgotten within the hour.</p>
    <p>You can delete your account at any time, from "Where your plan is kept" at the top of any page. Deleting removes your address, the copy of your plan, your passkeys and every sign-in, straight away. It takes your home out of every street group you joined at the same moment. The plan on your device stays, and "Export as a file" gives you a copy whenever you want one.</p>

    <h2 id="street">If you join a street group</h2>
    <p>A street group is neighbours telling each other what each could offer when the power, the water or the phones are off. Nobody is put in one: you start one, or you open an invitation a neighbour sent you, and you need an account for either.</p>
    <p>What you share in a group is locked in your browser before it leaves, with a key that travels only in the invitation and is kept only on the devices that opened it. We never have that key. So for a home in a group we keep two things: that your account is in the group, and a record we cannot read. We do not know what the group is called, your house number, your first name or anything you offer. We do know which accounts are in a group together, because that is how we decide who is given the group's records.</p>
    <p>Your neighbours see what you choose and nothing else. Everyone in the group sees your house number. Your first name, and each thing you offer, goes to nobody until you say otherwise, and then to the whole group or only to the homes you pick as your closer circle. Nobody is told who you picked. A group holds offers and never needs: there is nowhere to say who in a home is frail, what is kept there or when it is empty.</p>
    <p>Where the page says "your plan says you have this" beside an offer, your browser worked that out from the plan it keeps. Nothing was sent to find out, and nothing is offered until you choose it.</p>
    <p>A device that has opened a group keeps two things in its own storage: the group's key, and the street as it last saw it, so the street can still be read on the day there is no connection. Neither is sent anywhere, and the copy is shown only while you are signed in on that device.</p>
    <p>"Leave this group" removes your home and everything you shared from it at once, and the key and the copy from the device you leave on. The neighbour who has been in a group longest can remove a home, and the invitation that home had stops working when they do.</p>

    <h2 id="cookies">Cookies</h2>
    <p>Selficient sets no cookie that follows you or counts you, and there is no banner to click through because there is nothing to consent to. A page with a form on it sets one security cookie, which stops another website sending that form in your name; it holds a random value, says nothing about you and goes when you close the browser. If you sign in, a second cookie keeps you signed in on that device for ninety days, and a third, holding only the figure 1, tells the page to show that you are. While a sign-in code is on its way, one more holds your address, encrypted, for ten minutes, so the code only works in the browser that asked for it.</p>

    <h2 id="server">What our server sees</h2>
    <p>Like any website, the server records that a request happened, its address, the page asked for and the time, for a fortnight, to keep the site running and to stop abuse. It does not record your postcode, your answers, your photos or anything the model said. Photographs are re-encoded in memory and discarded the moment they have been read; they are never written to disk. If you send us a shop link under "Found it cheaper?", we keep the link and nothing about you.</p>
    <p>The people who run Selficient sign in to a reviewer that readers never see. Their accounts, and the record of their own sign-ins and the changes they make, are kept the way any staff system keeps them. That is about us, not about you.</p>

    <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
    <p>If you have not made an account, we hold nothing that identifies you, so there is nothing for us to show you, correct or delete; the data is in your hands already. If you have, what we hold is your address and the plan the site shows you: "Export as a file" downloads it, and deleting the account removes it. If you are in a street group we also hold that you are, and a record only your group can read: the group's page shows you all of it, and leaving removes it. If you think any of that is wrong in your case, write to <a href="mailto:@Selficient.Web.Pages.LegalCopy.ContactEmail">@Selficient.Web.Pages.LegalCopy.ContactEmail</a> and we will look.</p>

    <p class="disclosure">Reviewed @Selficient.Web.Pages.LegalCopy.Reviewed.ToString("d MMMM yyyy", gb). @Selficient.Web.Pages.LegalCopy.MadeBy</p>
  </article>
</div>
